 "version": 1,
 "id": "b7d3f9a2-5c81-4e60-9b47-1f8e62ad0c35",
 "title": "Uncolored Functions: Blocking I/O with WASM JSPI",
 "created_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
 "updated_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
 "shared_cargo_toml": null,
 "shared_source": null,
 "expand_code": true,
 "description": "Plain synchronous Rust that sleeps and calls HTTP APIs from inside iterator chains: JSPI suspends the whole WASM stack, so no async is needed anywhere. Requires Chrome or Edge 137+.",
 "tags": [
  "jspi",
  "blocking",
  "async",
  "http",
  "wasm"
 ],
 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "cell_banner",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "label": "Read this first",
   "source": "# Uncolored Functions\n\n> **Heads up: this notebook needs Chrome or Edge 137+.** It uses the WebAssembly JavaScript Promise Integration API (JSPI), which Firefox still keeps behind a flag and Safari has not shipped yet. On those browsers the code cells below will stop with a message saying exactly that.\n\nRust folks know the [\"What Color Is Your Function?\"](https://journal.stuffwithstuff.com/2015/02/01/what-color-is-your-function/) problem by heart: the moment one call does I/O, `async` spreads through every signature above it. Trait impls, iterator adapters, plain helper functions: none of them can await, so they can never do I/O. You restructure your program around the runtime.\n\n**JSPI deletes the color.** It is a [phase 4 WebAssembly proposal](https://github.com/WebAssembly/js-promise-integration) that lets the browser **suspend an entire WASM stack** while a JavaScript promise settles, then resume it exactly where it stopped. To the Rust code, a network call is just a function that takes a while, like it always was on a thread.\n\nEvery code cell in this notebook is **fully synchronous Rust**. Not one `.await`, not one `Future`. ironpad already runs async cells (see the [async HTTP notebook](/notebook/public/async-http.ironpad)); this one exists to show what happens when you stop needing them.",
   "order": 0,
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_sleep",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "label": "Sleeping without an executor",
   "source": "// Plain synchronous Rust. blocking::sleep_ms parks the WHOLE WASM stack\n// via JSPI while the browser runs a setTimeout; the tab stays perfectly\n// responsive because the suspended stack costs nothing.\nlet sw = Stopwatch::new();\n\nblocking::sleep_ms(400.0);\n\nlet elapsed = sw.elapsed_ms();\n\nMd(format!(\n    \"This cell just **blocked for {elapsed:.0} ms** in the middle of a \\\n     synchronous function. No async runtime, no callback, no `await` in \\\n     sight: the engine suspended the stack and resumed it on the same line.\",\n))",
   "order": 1,
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_how_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "label": "What just happened",
   "source": "## What just happened\n\nTwo `WebAssembly` API calls, both on the JavaScript side, both invisible from Rust. Here is the actual bridge, condensed. First, any import that might block is wrapped in **`WebAssembly.Suspending`**, which turns an ordinary async JS function into an import the engine may park a WASM stack on:\n\n```js\nimports.env.ironpad_blocking_sleep_ms = new WebAssembly.Suspending(\n  (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms))\n);\n\nimports.env.ironpad_blocking_fetch = new WebAssembly.Suspending(\n  async (urlPtr, urlLen) => {\n    const url = readUtf8(memory, urlPtr, urlLen);\n    const resp = await fetch(url);                        // stack is parked here\n    stash = new Uint8Array(await resp.arrayBuffer());\n    return stash.length;    // resuming the stack, this becomes the return value\n  }\n);\n```\n\nSecond, the entry point is invoked through **`WebAssembly.promising`**, which is what makes that suspension legal on this stack in the first place. The synchronous WASM export becomes an ordinary promise-returning function:\n\n```js\nconst main = WebAssembly.promising(instance.exports.cell_main);\nconst resultPtr = await main(inputPtr, inputLen);\n```\n\nOn the Rust side there is nothing exotic at all: `blocking::fetch_text` is a plain extern import plus a copy. The fetch reports how many bytes arrived while the stack was parked; the cell then allocates and a second, non-suspending import copies them in:\n\n```rust\n#[link(wasm_import_module = \"env\")]\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn ironpad_blocking_fetch(url_ptr: *const u8, url_len: u32) -> u32;\n    fn ironpad_blocking_fetch_ok() -> u32;\n    fn ironpad_blocking_read(buf_ptr: *mut u8, buf_len: u32) -> u32;\n}\n\npub fn fetch_bytes(url: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String> {\n    let len = unsafe { ironpad_blocking_fetch(url.as_ptr(), url.len() as u32) };\n    let ok = unsafe { ironpad_blocking_fetch_ok() } == 1;\n    let mut buf = vec![0u8; len as usize];\n    unsafe { ironpad_blocking_read(buf.as_mut_ptr(), len) };\n    if ok { Ok(buf) } else { Err(String::from_utf8_lossy(&buf).into_owned()) }\n}\n```\n\nNote what is missing: no compiler flags, no special codegen, no async runtime linked into the module. The same `.wasm` binary runs unmodified in any engine that ships JSPI. **The async machinery moved out of your program and into the VM**, which is where threads always kept it on native targets.\n\nSleeping is a parlor trick, though. The point of deleting function colors is doing I/O from places `async` cannot reach.",
   "order": 2,
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_iter",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "label": "I/O in the middle of an iterator",
   "source": "// Three HTTP GETs issued lazily from inside a plain iterator adapter.\n// There is no async block, no join_all, no collecting futures: each call\n// parks the stack mid-`map` while the browser fetches, then resumes.\nlet rows: Vec<Vec<String>> = [\"welcome\", \"tutorial\", \"cannon\"]\n    .iter()\n    .map(|stem| {\n        let url = format!(\"/notebooks/{stem}.ironpad\");\n        match blocking::fetch_json::<serde_json::Value>(&url) {\n            Ok(nb) => vec![\n                format!(\"{stem}.ironpad\"),\n                nb[\"title\"].as_str().unwrap_or(\"?\").to_string(),\n                nb[\"cells\"].as_array().map_or(0, Vec::len).to_string(),\n            ],\n            Err(e) => vec![format!(\"{stem}.ironpad\"), format!(\"error: {e}\"), \"-\".to_string()],\n        }\n    })\n    .collect();\n\nTable::new(vec![\"file\", \"title\", \"cells\"], rows)",
   "order": 3,
   "cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nserde_json = \"1\""
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_color_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "label": "Try writing that with async",
   "source": "## Try writing that with async\n\nThe colored version of the last cell is a small essay. `Iterator::map` takes a plain closure, so an `async` closure needs `futures::stream::iter(...).then(...).collect()` plus a `StreamExt` import, or you give up on iterators and hand-roll a loop inside an `async` block, and either way the enclosing function signature changes, and then its caller's does. The I/O reshapes code that never touches the network.\n\nHere the closure just... calls a function. The iterator has no idea the network was involved, and neither does anything else on the stack. That is what \"uncolored\" buys: **composition with every synchronous abstraction you already have**, traits and closures and iterators included.",
   "order": 4,
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_github",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "label": "A real API, still no await",
   "source": "// A real cross-origin API, still zero async. api.github.com sends CORS\n// headers, so the browser's fetch is happy to oblige. If you are offline\n// or rate-limited, the Err arm reports it gracefully instead of trapping.\nlet sw = Stopwatch::new();\n\nmatch blocking::fetch_json::<serde_json::Value>(\"https://api.github.com/repos/rust-lang/rust\") {\n    Ok(repo) => {\n        let ms = sw.elapsed_ms();\n        let count = |key: &str| {\n            repo[key]\n                .as_u64()\n                .map_or_else(|| \"?\".to_string(), |v| v.to_string())\n        };\n        CellOutput::from(Table::new(\n            vec![\"rust-lang/rust\", \"count\"],\n            vec![\n                vec![\"stars\".to_string(), count(\"stargazers_count\")],\n                vec![\"forks\".to_string(), count(\"forks_count\")],\n                vec![\"open issues\".to_string(), count(\"open_issues_count\")],\n                vec![\"round trip (ms)\".to_string(), format!(\"{ms:.0}\")],\n            ],\n        ))\n    }\n    Err(e) => CellOutput::text(format!(\n        \"GitHub said no ({e}). Offline or rate-limited; the point stands: \\\n         this error came back through a plain synchronous call.\"\n    )),\n}",
   "order": 5,
   "cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nserde_json = \"1\""
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_notes",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "label": "Support and sharp edges",
   "source": "## Support and sharp edges\n\n| Engine | JSPI status |\n|---|---|\n| Chrome / Edge | **shipped, 137+** (mid-2025) |\n| Firefox | implemented, behind a flag through 155 |\n| Safari | in development (objection withdrawn late 2025) |\n\nJSPI is one of the [Interop 2026 focus areas](https://web.dev/blog/interop-2026), so the missing engines have a public deadline of sorts. Until then, ironpad detects the `ironpad_blocking_*` imports at load time and fails **before execution** with a plain-language message on unsupported browsers, rather than letting the cell trap.\n\nTwo rules to keep out of the weeds:\n\n- **Sync cells only.** A cell containing `.await` compiles to an async entry point, which cannot be entered through `WebAssembly.promising`. Pick a lane per cell: `blocking::*` or `.await`, never both.\n- **Fetches follow browser rules.** Same-origin is always fine; cross-origin needs CORS from the server, exactly like the async `http` module.",
   "order": 6,
   "cargo_toml": null
  }
 ]
}
